Here's a simple JavaScript script that makes the cursor bigger:

```javascript
// Function to increase cursor size
function increaseCursorSize() {
    // Set cursor size to 20 pixels
    document.body.style.cursor = 'crosshair';

    // Create a new cursor with a size of 20 pixels
    var cursor = document.createElement('div');
    cursor.style.position = 'fixed';
    cursor.style.top = '0px';
    cursor.style.left = '0px';
    cursor.style.width = '20px';
    cursor.style.height = '20px';
    cursor.style.background = 'black';
    cursor.style.borderRadius = '50%';
    cursor.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
    cursor.style.transition = 'transform 1s';
    cursor.style.transform = 'scale(2)';
    document.body.appendChild(cursor);

    // Move the cursor to the top-left corner of the screen
    cursor.style.transform = 'scale(2) translate(-10px, -10px)';
}

// Call the function
increaseCursorSize();

// Function to decrease cursor size
function decreaseCursorSize() {
    // Remove the cursor from the DOM
    var cursor = document.querySelector('div.cursor');
    if (cursor) {
        cursor.remove();
    }
}
```

However, if you want to make the cursor size larger in a cross-platform way without using JavaScript, you can add the following code to your CSS file:

```css
body {
    cursor: crosshair;
}
cursor {
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: black;
    transition: transform 1s;
}
```

To change the cursor size, you can simply update the `width` and `height` properties in the CSS rule.

Remember to add the CSS code to your CSS file and not to the HTML file. 

Note: The `cursor` property in CSS only works for mouse cursors, not for other input devices like touchscreens or pens.

